## Amendment to the ruling above (skills seat, 15:3xZ) — "drop `props`, `events`" is too strong; measure each member against its real reader, not only the TS interface

The published `skills/objectui/rules/protocol.md` fence (the same shape) says `props` is the `element:*` config envelope under its own rule 「Keys Live on the Node」, i.e. a key a real reader accepts on the wire even though the TS `BaseSchema` does not list it. So the test for a member is not "is it on `BaseSchema`" but "does a reader in `packages/**` accept it on a node of this kind, and is that documented by a rule in the same file". Option 2 stands with that test:

- a member no reader accepts and no rule names goes;
- a member a reader accepts stays, with a comment that names the node kind it is accepted on;
- `events` is #7945's question (awaiting the maintainer — whether the `events` bag is the action system at all): the flight does not pre-empt it; it only replaces the unexported element type `ActionDef` with the exported `ActionSchema` and leaves the line's semantics to that ruling;
- `hidden` / `disabled` / `children` take the real declarations; the abridged note points at `packages/types/src/base.ts`.

The same amended test applies to #8065's two fences (flight K, dispatched 15:3xZ).

